---
title: "SSRM Aggregation"
enterprise: true
---

With Aggregation in the SSRM, the server calculates the aggregated values and returns them on the
[group rows](./server-side-model-grouping/).

In the example below, note the following:

- **Gold** and **Silver** are aggregated with `sum`, and **Bronze** with `max`.
- Each group row shows the aggregated values of its rows, as calculated by the server.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Aggregation" name="aggregation" /%}

## Enabling Aggregation

Set the `aggFunc` column definition property on each column to aggregate:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'country', rowGroup: true },
        { field: 'gold', aggFunc: 'sum' },
        { field: 'bronze', aggFunc: 'max' },
    ]
}
```

Use the names of functions your server implements, such as `'sum'`, `'min'`, `'max'`, `'avg'` and `'count'`. The
request sends `aggFunc` exactly as it is set on the column, so it must be a string. To let users choose the aggregated
columns and their functions, see [Configuring via the UI](./aggregation-columns/#configuring-via-the-ui).

## Aggregated Columns in the Request

The request lists the aggregated columns in `valueCols`, beside the grouping properties described in
[SSRM Row Grouping](./server-side-model-grouping/#grouping-rows-on-the-server):

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="IServerSideGetRowsRequest" names=["valueCols"] config={"description": ""} /%}

For example, the top-level request of the example above is:

```js
{
    rowGroupCols: [
        { id: 'country', field: 'country', displayName: 'Country' },
        { id: 'sport', field: 'sport', displayName: 'Sport' },
    ],
    groupKeys: [],
    valueCols: [
        { id: 'gold', field: 'gold', displayName: 'Gold', aggFunc: 'sum' },
        { id: 'silver', field: 'silver', displayName: 'Silver', aggFunc: 'sum' },
        { id: 'bronze', field: 'bronze', displayName: 'Bronze', aggFunc: 'max' },
    ],
    // ...
}
```

## Returning Aggregated Values on Group Rows

Return each aggregated value on the group row under the column's field, beside the group key. The grid reads it as it
reads any other value, through the column's `field` or `valueGetter`. Leaf rows are returned as they are. The server
can answer the request above with:

```js
params.success({
    rowData: [
        { country: 'United States', gold: 552, silver: 440, bronze: 3 },
        { country: 'Russia', gold: 234, silver: 221, bronze: 3 },
        // ...
    ],
    rowCount: 110,
});
```

## Updating Aggregated Values After Data Changes

The grid does not calculate aggregated values in the SSRM, so it does not update them when leaf rows change.
Recalculate them on the server, then send the updated group rows with a
[transaction](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/), or reload the group with
[`refreshServerSide`](./server-side-model-updating-refresh/).

## Sorting and Filtering Aggregated Columns

Sorting and filtering are applied by the server, as described in [SSRM Sorting](./server-side-model-sorting/) and
[SSRM Filtering](./server-side-model-filtering/). With aggregation:

- Sorting an aggregated column orders the group rows by their aggregated values. The grid reloads every group level, as
  every group row has a value for the column. See [Sorting All Levels](./server-side-model-sorting/#sorting-all-levels).
- The `sortModel` can hold the ID of the group column, such as `ag-Grid-AutoColumn`, which is not a field of the data.
  The examples on this page ignore column IDs the data does not have.
- The examples on this page filter leaf rows before aggregating, so group rows show the aggregated values of the rows
  that pass the filter.
- Filtering an aggregated column reloads every group level. See [Filtering with Row Groups](./server-side-model-grouping/#filtering-with-row-groups).

## Group Total Rows

To enable [Group Total Rows](./aggregation-total-rows/), set the `groupTotalRow` property to 'top' or 'bottom'.
A group total row shows the aggregated values of its group row, so the server returns nothing extra for it.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Group Totals" name="group-footer" /%}

Group total rows can also be used with `groupDisplayType='multipleColumns'`, as demonstrated in the example below.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Multiple Group Columns and Footers" name="group-footer-multiple-cols" /%}

## Grand Total Row

To display a grand total row, set the `grandTotalRow` property to `'top'`, `'bottom'`, `'pinnedTop'`, or `'pinnedBottom'`. The grand total row is supported for both flat grids and grids with row grouping.

### Providing Grand Total Data

When `grandTotalRow` is set, the `needsGrandTotal` hint on the `getRows` params is `true` for root-level requests
that have no cached grand total: on first load, and after a filter or aggregation change purges the cache. A sort
change keeps the grand total, so `needsGrandTotal` stays `false`. The server can also send the grand total on every
request.

The `grandTotalData` field of the `success` params controls the grand total row:

- Pass the **grand total data object** to set or update it.
- Pass `null` to remove it.
- Omit it, or pass `undefined`, to keep the cached grand total. Paged block requests can then return data rows without
  sending the grand total again.

While the grid has no rows, the grand total row is not shown; it returns with the rows. Set `alwaysShowGrandTotalRow` to `true` to keep it shown with the `grandTotalData` the server returns; the grid still shows its no-rows or no-matching-rows overlay.

In the example below, the server recalculates the grand total when a filter changes, and changing an aggregation
function in the column menu requests both the rows and the grand total again.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Grand Total via getRows" name="grand-total-getrows" exampleHeight=590 /%}

With `getRowId`, a row in `rowData` with the ID `GRAND_TOTAL_ROW_ID` (`'rowGroupFooter_ROOT_NODE_ID'`) is also
treated as the grand total. The `grandTotalData` field takes priority.

### Updating the Grand Total Row via Transactions

The grand total row can be updated, added, or removed via `applyServerSideTransaction`:

- **Update**: include the grand total data in `update`. `getRowId` must return `GRAND_TOTAL_ROW_ID`
  (`'rowGroupFooter_ROOT_NODE_ID'`) for it.
- **Add**: include the grand total data in `add`. An existing grand total is updated.
- **Remove**: include a row whose `getRowId` returns `GRAND_TOTAL_ROW_ID` in `remove`.

Use this when the grand total comes from a separate endpoint, such as a slower totals query that can be cancelled on
its own.

The example below does this on a flat grid. Each `getRows` call fetches only the data rows. When the grid sets
`needsGrandTotal`, a separate request for the grand total starts. While it runs, a transaction removes the current
grand total so stale values are not shown, and an `add` transaction applies the response. A request ID discards a
response that arrives after a newer request started.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Grand Total with Transactions" name="grand-total-transactions" exampleHeight=590 /%}

### Accessing Grand Total and Group Total Rows

Get the grand total row and group total rows by ID with `api.getRowNode()`. Two constants exported from
`ag-grid-community` define the IDs:

- `GRAND_TOTAL_ROW_ID` (`'rowGroupFooter_ROOT_NODE_ID'`) — the ID of the grand total row.
- `GROUP_TOTAL_ROW_ID_PREFIX` (`'rowGroupFooter_'`) — the prefix for group total row IDs. A group total row ID is `GROUP_TOTAL_ROW_ID_PREFIX + groupRowNode.id`.

```js
// Retrieve the grand total row node
const grandTotalNode = api.getRowNode(GRAND_TOTAL_ROW_ID);

// Retrieve a group total row node (e.g. for group "Ireland")
const groupTotalNode = api.getRowNode(GROUP_TOTAL_ROW_ID_PREFIX + groupRowNode.id);
```

While the grid has no rows there is no grand total row, so `api.getRowNode(GRAND_TOTAL_ROW_ID)` returns `undefined`, unless `alwaysShowGrandTotalRow` is set and the server returned `grandTotalData`.

## Grouping and Aggregating the Same Column

To group by a column and also aggregate it, each group row needs two values for that column: the group key and the
aggregate. Have the server return the group key under the column's field, as for any other grouped column, and the
aggregate under a separate field. Then read the aggregate with the `aggValueGetter` column definition property. One
entry in the default column definition covers every column:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    defaultColDef: {
        // the server returns { gold: 4, sum_gold: 12 } for the group row of gold 4
        aggValueGetter: (params) => {
            const aggValue = params.data?.[`${params.aggFunc}_${params.colDef.field}`];
            return aggValue !== undefined ? aggValue : params.value;
        },
    },
}
```

The grid calls `aggValueGetter` when it reads the value of an aggregated column on a group row, a group total row or
the grand total row. `params.value` is the value read from the row data, and `params.aggFunc` is the column's
aggregation function, as sent in `valueCols`. The group key is always read from the column's field,
so the group column and the `groupKeys` of later requests are unchanged. To change how the key is read, see
[Grouping by Nested Object Fields](./server-side-model-grouping/#grouping-by-nested-object-fields).

{% apiDocumentation source="column-properties/properties.json" section="aggregation" names=["aggValueGetter"] /%}

{% note %}
`${params.aggFunc}_${params.colDef.field}` is the naming this example's server uses. Build the key in `aggValueGetter`
from whatever names your server returns, such as a nested `data.aggs.gold` object or a fixed field per column.
{% /note %}

By default, sorting the group column and sorting the aggregated column send the same request, so the server chooses
whether that group level is ordered by its key or by its aggregate. Grouping a column also hides it by default. To keep it visible, see
[Prevent User Grouping from Hiding Columns](./grouping-group-panel/#prevent-user-grouping-from-hiding-columns).

The example below groups by **Gold**, aggregates it with `sum` and sorts it ascending. Note the following:

- The server returns the aggregate of a grouped column as `<aggFunc>_<field>`, such as `sum_gold`, so the getter also
  works when other columns are grouped and aggregated from the Columns Tool Panel.
- Aggregates of columns that are not grouped are returned under their field, and the getter returns `params.value`.
- The server orders a group level sorted by its own column by key.
- **Gold** appears in both Row Groups and Values in the Columns Tool Panel.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Grouping and Aggregating the Same Column" name="group-agg-same-column" exampleHeight=590 /%}

